<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<article xmlns="http://docbook.org/ns/docbook" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" version="5.0" xml:lang="en-US" role="numbered">
  <info>
    <title>From Basel to Gauss: Sums, Products and Integrals</title>
    <author>
      <orgname>Maxprograms</orgname>
    </author>
    <pubdate>14 November 2026</pubdate>
    <copyright>
      <year>2026</year>
      <holder>Maxprograms</holder>
    </copyright>
    <abstract>
      <para>A short walk through classical results of analysis: telescoping sums, the geometric
        series, Euler’s product for the sine, the Gaussian integral, an eigenvalue problem and
        the binomial theorem. Each result is stated, proved in a few lines and checked against
        numbers.</para>
    </abstract>
  </info>

  <section xml:id="sec-intro">
    <title>Introduction</title>
    <para>In 1734 Leonhard Euler found the exact value of the sum of the reciprocals of the squares
      of the natural numbers, a problem that had resisted the best mathematicians for almost a
      century:</para>
    <informalequation xml:id="eq-basel">
      <m:math display="block">
        <m:mtable width="100%" columnwidth="fit auto fit" columnalign="center center right">
          <m:mtr>
            <m:mtd/>
            <m:mtd>
              <m:mrow><m:mi>ζ</m:mi><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo>)</m:mo></m:mrow>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:munderover>
                <m:mo>∑</m:mo>
                <m:mrow><m:mi>n</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:mrow>
                <m:mi>∞</m:mi>
              </m:munderover>
              <m:mfrac>
                <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                <m:msup><m:mi>n</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup>
              </m:mfrac>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mfrac>
                <m:msup><m:mi>π</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup>
                <m:mn>6</m:mn>
              </m:mfrac>
            </m:mtd>
            <m:mtd class="eqnum"/>
          </m:mtr>
        </m:mtable>
      </m:math>
    </informalequation>
    <para>The series converges, because its terms behave like
      <inlineequation><m:math><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo>/</m:mo><m:msup><m:mi>n</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup></m:math></inlineequation>
      for large <inlineequation><m:math><m:mi>n</m:mi></m:math></inlineequation>, but it converges
      slowly: after <inlineequation><m:math><m:mi>n</m:mi></m:math></inlineequation> terms the
      error is about
      <inlineequation><m:math><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo>/</m:mo><m:mi>n</m:mi></m:math></inlineequation>,
      as the table in <xref linkend="sec-numbers" xrefstyle="section"/> shows. This article collects
      proofs of nearby facts that use the same tools, from the first year of calculus to a little
      linear algebra. Equation <xref linkend="eq-basel" xrefstyle="equation"/> is the destination of the walk.</para>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-telescoping">
    <title>Telescoping sums</title>
    <example role="theorem">
      <title>Theorem 1</title>
      <para>For every natural number
        <inlineequation><m:math><m:mi>n</m:mi><m:mo>≥</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:math></inlineequation>,</para>
      <informalequation xml:id="eq-telescoping">
        <m:math display="block">
          <m:mtable width="100%" columnwidth="fit auto fit" columnalign="center center right">
            <m:mtr>
              <m:mtd/>
              <m:mtd>
                <m:munderover>
                  <m:mo>∑</m:mo>
                  <m:mrow><m:mi>k</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:mrow>
                  <m:mi>n</m:mi>
                </m:munderover>
                <m:mfrac>
                  <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                  <m:mrow><m:mi>k</m:mi><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mi>k</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo>)</m:mo></m:mrow>
                </m:mfrac>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mfrac>
                  <m:mi>n</m:mi>
                  <m:mrow><m:mi>n</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:mrow>
                </m:mfrac>
              </m:mtd>
              <m:mtd class="eqnum"/>
            </m:mtr>
          </m:mtable>
        </m:math>
      </informalequation>
    </example>
    <para role="proof"><emphasis>Proof.</emphasis> Each term can be split into two fractions:</para>
    <informalequation xml:id="eq-partial-fractions">
      <m:math display="block">
        <m:mtable width="100%" columnwidth="fit auto fit" columnalign="center center right">
          <m:mtr>
            <m:mtd/>
            <m:mtd>
              <m:mfrac>
                <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                <m:mrow><m:mi>k</m:mi><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mi>k</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo>)</m:mo></m:mrow>
              </m:mfrac>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mfrac><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mi>k</m:mi></m:mfrac>
              <m:mo>−</m:mo>
              <m:mfrac>
                <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                <m:mrow><m:mi>k</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:mrow>
              </m:mfrac>
            </m:mtd>
            <m:mtd class="eqnum"/>
          </m:mtr>
        </m:mtable>
      </m:math>
    </informalequation>
    <para role="proof end">so the sum telescopes: every term cancels with the next one, and only the
      first and the last survive. What remains is
      <inlineequation><m:math>
        <m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo>−</m:mo>
        <m:mfrac><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mrow><m:mi>n</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac>
        <m:mo>=</m:mo>
        <m:mi>n</m:mi><m:mo>/</m:mo><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mi>n</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo>)</m:mo>
      </m:math></inlineequation>.</para>
    <para>Letting
      <inlineequation><m:math><m:mi>n</m:mi><m:mo>→</m:mo><m:mi>∞</m:mi></m:math></inlineequation>
      gives a sum of exactly 1. The same idea bounds the series of <xref linkend="eq-basel" xrefstyle="equation"/>: for
      <inlineequation><m:math><m:mi>k</m:mi><m:mo>≥</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:math></inlineequation>
      we have
      <inlineequation><m:math>
        <m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo>/</m:mo><m:msup><m:mi>k</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup>
        <m:mo>&lt;</m:mo>
        <m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo>/</m:mo><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mi>k</m:mi><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mi>k</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo>)</m:mo><m:mo>)</m:mo>
      </m:math></inlineequation>, and the second sum telescopes as above, so</para>
    <informalequation xml:id="eq-bound">
      <m:math display="block">
        <m:mtable width="100%" columnwidth="fit auto fit" columnalign="center center right">
          <m:mtr>
            <m:mtd/>
            <m:mtd>
              <m:munderover>
                <m:mo>∑</m:mo>
                <m:mrow><m:mi>k</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:mrow>
                <m:mi>n</m:mi>
              </m:munderover>
              <m:mfrac>
                <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                <m:msup><m:mi>k</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup>
              </m:mfrac>
              <m:mo>&lt;</m:mo>
              <m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mi>n</m:mi></m:mfrac>
              <m:mo>&lt;</m:mo>
              <m:mn>2</m:mn>
            </m:mtd>
            <m:mtd class="eqnum"/>
          </m:mtr>
        </m:mtable>
      </m:math>
    </informalequation>
    <para>The partial sums grow and stay below 2, so the series converges.</para>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-geometric">
    <title>The geometric series</title>
    <para>For a real number
      <inlineequation><m:math><m:mi>r</m:mi><m:mo>≠</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:math></inlineequation>
      the partial sums of the powers of
      <inlineequation><m:math><m:mi>r</m:mi></m:math></inlineequation> have a closed form:</para>
    <informalequation xml:id="eq-geometric">
      <m:math display="block">
        <m:mtable width="100%" columnwidth="fit auto fit" columnalign="center center right">
          <m:mtr>
            <m:mtd/>
            <m:mtd>
              <m:apply>
                <m:eq/>
                <m:apply>
                  <m:sum/>
                  <m:bvar><m:ci>k</m:ci></m:bvar>
                  <m:lowlimit><m:cn>0</m:cn></m:lowlimit>
                  <m:uplimit><m:ci>n</m:ci></m:uplimit>
                  <m:apply><m:power/><m:ci>r</m:ci><m:ci>k</m:ci></m:apply>
                </m:apply>
                <m:apply>
                  <m:divide/>
                  <m:apply>
                    <m:minus/>
                    <m:cn>1</m:cn>
                    <m:apply><m:power/><m:ci>r</m:ci><m:apply><m:plus/><m:ci>n</m:ci><m:cn>1</m:cn></m:apply></m:apply>
                  </m:apply>
                  <m:apply><m:minus/><m:cn>1</m:cn><m:ci>r</m:ci></m:apply>
                </m:apply>
              </m:apply>
            </m:mtd>
            <m:mtd class="eqnum"/>
          </m:mtr>
        </m:mtable>
      </m:math>
    </informalequation>
    <para>To see this, multiply the sum by
      <inlineequation><m:math><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mi>r</m:mi></m:math></inlineequation>:
      all the intermediate powers cancel in pairs and only
      <inlineequation><m:math><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:msup><m:mi>r</m:mi><m:mrow><m:mi>n</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:mrow></m:msup></m:math></inlineequation>
      is left. When
      <inlineequation><m:math><m:mrow><m:mo>|</m:mo><m:mi>r</m:mi><m:mo>|</m:mo></m:mrow><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:math></inlineequation>
      the power
      <inlineequation><m:math><m:msup><m:mi>r</m:mi><m:mrow><m:mi>n</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:mrow></m:msup></m:math></inlineequation>
      tends to 0, and the series has a sum:</para>
    <informalequation xml:id="eq-geometric-series">
      <m:math display="block">
        <m:mtable width="100%" columnwidth="fit auto fit" columnalign="center center right">
          <m:mtr>
            <m:mtd/>
            <m:mtd>
              <m:munderover>
                <m:mo>∑</m:mo>
                <m:mrow><m:mi>k</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mrow>
                <m:mi>∞</m:mi>
              </m:munderover>
              <m:msup><m:mi>r</m:mi><m:mi>k</m:mi></m:msup>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mfrac>
                <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                <m:mrow><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mi>r</m:mi></m:mrow>
              </m:mfrac>
              <m:mo>,</m:mo>
              <m:mspace width="1.5em"/>
              <m:mrow><m:mo>|</m:mo><m:mi>r</m:mi><m:mo>|</m:mo></m:mrow>
              <m:mo>&lt;</m:mo>
              <m:mn>1</m:mn>
            </m:mtd>
            <m:mtd class="eqnum"/>
          </m:mtr>
        </m:mtable>
      </m:math>
    </informalequation>
    <para>With
      <inlineequation><m:math><m:mi>r</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo>/</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:math></inlineequation>
      this says that
      <inlineequation><m:math><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo>/</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo>/</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo>/</m:mo><m:mn>8</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mo>⋯</m:mo></m:math></inlineequation>
      adds up to 2, the picture of a segment that is halved again and again.</para>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-euler">
    <title>Euler’s product for the sine</title>
    <para>Euler’s idea was to treat the sine as a polynomial of infinite degree whose roots are the
      integers. Written as a product over its roots, and scaled so that its value at 0 is 1, it
      becomes<footnote><para>The product converges for every real
      <inlineequation><m:math><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:math></inlineequation>. Taking
      <inlineequation><m:math><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo>/</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:math></inlineequation>
      gives the product of Wallis,
      <inlineequation><m:math>
        <m:mi>π</m:mi><m:mo>/</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo>
        <m:munderover>
          <m:mo>∏</m:mo>
          <m:mrow><m:mi>n</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:mrow>
          <m:mi>∞</m:mi>
        </m:munderover>
        <m:mfrac>
          <m:mrow><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:msup><m:mi>n</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup></m:mrow>
          <m:mrow><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:msup><m:mi>n</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:mrow>
        </m:mfrac>
      </m:math></inlineequation>. A rigorous proof of the product needs the factorization theorem
      of Weierstrass.</para></footnote></para>
    <informalequation xml:id="eq-product">
      <m:math display="block">
        <m:mtable width="100%" columnwidth="fit auto fit" columnalign="center center right">
          <m:mtr>
            <m:mtd/>
            <m:mtd>
              <m:mfrac>
                <m:mrow><m:mi>sin</m:mi><m:mo>&#x2061;</m:mo><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mi>π</m:mi><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>)</m:mo></m:mrow>
                <m:mrow><m:mi>π</m:mi><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow>
              </m:mfrac>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:munderover>
                <m:mo>∏</m:mo>
                <m:mrow><m:mi>n</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:mrow>
                <m:mi>∞</m:mi>
              </m:munderover>
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mo>(</m:mo>
                <m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo>−</m:mo>
                <m:mfrac>
                  <m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup>
                  <m:msup><m:mi>n</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup>
                </m:mfrac>
                <m:mo>)</m:mo>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:mtd>
            <m:mtd class="eqnum"/>
          </m:mtr>
        </m:mtable>
      </m:math>
    </informalequation>
    <para>On the other hand, the Taylor series of the sine, divided by
      <inlineequation><m:math><m:mi>π</m:mi><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:math></inlineequation>, gives</para>
    <informalequation xml:id="eq-taylor">
      <m:math display="block">
        <m:mtable width="100%" columnwidth="fit auto fit" columnalign="center center right">
          <m:mtr>
            <m:mtd/>
            <m:mtd>
              <m:mfrac>
                <m:mrow><m:mi>sin</m:mi><m:mo>&#x2061;</m:mo><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mi>π</m:mi><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>)</m:mo></m:mrow>
                <m:mrow><m:mi>π</m:mi><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow>
              </m:mfrac>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:munderover>
                <m:mo>∑</m:mo>
                <m:mrow><m:mi>k</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mrow>
                <m:mi>∞</m:mi>
              </m:munderover>
              <m:mfrac>
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:msup><m:mrow><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mrow><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mo>)</m:mo></m:mrow><m:mi>k</m:mi></m:msup>
                  <m:msup><m:mrow><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mi>π</m:mi><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>)</m:mo></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mi>k</m:mi></m:mrow></m:msup>
                </m:mrow>
                <m:mrow><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mi>k</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo>)</m:mo><m:mo>!</m:mo></m:mrow>
              </m:mfrac>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo>−</m:mo>
              <m:mfrac>
                <m:mrow><m:msup><m:mi>π</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup></m:mrow>
                <m:mn>6</m:mn>
              </m:mfrac>
              <m:mo>+</m:mo>
              <m:mfrac>
                <m:mrow><m:msup><m:mi>π</m:mi><m:mn>4</m:mn></m:msup><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>4</m:mn></m:msup></m:mrow>
                <m:mn>120</m:mn>
              </m:mfrac>
              <m:mo>−</m:mo>
              <m:mo>⋯</m:mo>
            </m:mtd>
            <m:mtd class="eqnum"/>
          </m:mtr>
        </m:mtable>
      </m:math>
    </informalequation>
    <para>Compare the coefficients of
      <inlineequation><m:math><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup></m:math></inlineequation>
      in <xref linkend="eq-product" xrefstyle="equation"/> and <xref linkend="eq-taylor" xrefstyle="equation"/>. Multiplying out the
      product, the only way to get
      <inlineequation><m:math><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup></m:math></inlineequation>
      is to take one factor
      <inlineequation><m:math><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>/</m:mo><m:msup><m:mi>n</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup></m:math></inlineequation>
      and 1 from all the others, so the coefficient is
      <inlineequation><m:math>
        <m:mo>−</m:mo>
        <m:munderover>
          <m:mo>∑</m:mo>
          <m:mrow><m:mi>n</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:mrow>
          <m:mi>∞</m:mi>
        </m:munderover>
        <m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo>/</m:mo><m:msup><m:mi>n</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup>
      </m:math></inlineequation>. In the series it is
      <inlineequation><m:math><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:msup><m:mi>π</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>/</m:mo><m:mn>6</m:mn></m:math></inlineequation>.
      Equating them gives <xref linkend="eq-basel" xrefstyle="equation"/>.</para>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-gauss">
    <title>The Gaussian integral</title>
    <example role="theorem">
      <title>Theorem 2</title>
      <para>The area under the bell curve is</para>
      <informalequation xml:id="eq-gauss">
        <m:math display="block">
          <m:mtable width="100%" columnwidth="fit auto fit" columnalign="center center right">
            <m:mtr>
              <m:mtd/>
              <m:mtd>
                <m:msubsup>
                  <m:mo>∫</m:mo>
                  <m:mrow><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mi>∞</m:mi></m:mrow>
                  <m:mi>∞</m:mi>
                </m:msubsup>
                <m:msup><m:mi>e</m:mi><m:mrow><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup></m:mrow></m:msup>
                <m:mspace width="0.2em"/>
                <m:mi>d</m:mi><m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:msqrt><m:mi>π</m:mi></m:msqrt>
              </m:mtd>
              <m:mtd class="eqnum"/>
            </m:mtr>
          </m:mtable>
        </m:math>
      </informalequation>
    </example>
    <para role="proof"><emphasis>Proof.</emphasis> Call the integral
      <inlineequation><m:math><m:mi>I</m:mi></m:math></inlineequation>. The trick is to square it and
      to read the product of two integrals as an integral over the plane:</para>
    <informalequation xml:id="eq-plane">
      <m:math display="block">
        <m:mtable width="100%" columnwidth="fit auto fit" columnalign="center center right">
          <m:mtr>
            <m:mtd/>
            <m:mtd>
              <m:msup><m:mi>I</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:msub>
                <m:mo>∬</m:mo>
                <m:msup><m:mi mathvariant="double-struck">R</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup>
              </m:msub>
              <m:msup>
                <m:mi>e</m:mi>
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mo>(</m:mo>
                  <m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:msup><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup>
                  <m:mo>)</m:mo>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:msup>
              <m:mspace width="0.2em"/>
              <m:mi>d</m:mi><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mspace width="0.15em"/><m:mi>d</m:mi><m:mi>y</m:mi>
            </m:mtd>
            <m:mtd class="eqnum"/>
          </m:mtr>
        </m:mtable>
      </m:math>
    </informalequation>
    <para role="proof">In polar coordinates the area element is
      <inlineequation><m:math><m:mi>r</m:mi><m:mspace width="0.2em"/><m:mi>d</m:mi><m:mi>r</m:mi><m:mspace width="0.15em"/><m:mi>d</m:mi><m:mi>θ</m:mi></m:math></inlineequation>,
      and the integral becomes elementary:</para>
    <informalequation xml:id="eq-polar">
      <m:math display="block">
        <m:mtable width="100%" columnwidth="fit auto fit" columnalign="center center right">
          <m:mtr>
            <m:mtd/>
            <m:mtd>
              <m:msup><m:mi>I</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:msubsup>
                <m:mo>∫</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mrow><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mi>π</m:mi></m:mrow>
              </m:msubsup>
              <m:msubsup>
                <m:mo>∫</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mi>∞</m:mi>
              </m:msubsup>
              <m:msup><m:mi>e</m:mi><m:mrow><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:msup><m:mi>r</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup></m:mrow></m:msup>
              <m:mspace width="0.2em"/>
              <m:mi>r</m:mi><m:mspace width="0.2em"/><m:mi>d</m:mi><m:mi>r</m:mi><m:mspace width="0.15em"/><m:mi>d</m:mi><m:mi>θ</m:mi>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mi>π</m:mi><m:mo>⋅</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mfrac>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mi>π</m:mi>
            </m:mtd>
            <m:mtd class="eqnum"/>
          </m:mtr>
        </m:mtable>
      </m:math>
    </informalequation>
    <para role="proof end">Since <inlineequation><m:math><m:mi>I</m:mi></m:math></inlineequation> is
      positive,
      <inlineequation><m:math><m:mi>I</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:msqrt><m:mi>π</m:mi></m:msqrt></m:math></inlineequation>.</para>
    <para>Statisticians use this result every day. Dividing the bell curve by its area, and
      allowing a mean <inlineequation><m:math><m:mi>μ</m:mi></m:math></inlineequation> and a standard
      deviation <inlineequation><m:math><m:mi>σ</m:mi></m:math></inlineequation>, gives the density
      of the normal distribution, whose total probability is 1 as it should be:</para>
    <informalequation xml:id="eq-normal">
      <m:math display="block">
        <m:mtable width="100%" columnwidth="fit auto fit" columnalign="center center right">
          <m:mtr>
            <m:mtd/>
            <m:mtd>
              <m:msubsup>
                <m:mo>∫</m:mo>
                <m:mrow><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mi>∞</m:mi></m:mrow>
                <m:mi>∞</m:mi>
              </m:msubsup>
              <m:mfrac>
                <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                <m:mrow><m:mi>σ</m:mi><m:msqrt><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mi>π</m:mi></m:msqrt></m:mrow>
              </m:mfrac>
              <m:msup>
                <m:mi>e</m:mi>
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                  <m:mfrac>
                    <m:msup><m:mrow><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mi>μ</m:mi><m:mo>)</m:mo></m:mrow><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup>
                    <m:mrow><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:msup><m:mi>σ</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup></m:mrow>
                  </m:mfrac>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:msup>
              <m:mspace width="0.2em"/>
              <m:mi>d</m:mi><m:mi>x</m:mi>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mn>1</m:mn>
            </m:mtd>
            <m:mtd class="eqnum"/>
          </m:mtr>
        </m:mtable>
      </m:math>
    </informalequation>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-matrix">
    <title>A small eigenvalue problem</title>
    <para>Linear algebra has its own sums. Take the symmetric matrix</para>
    <informalequation xml:id="eq-matrix">
      <m:math display="block">
        <m:mtable width="100%" columnwidth="fit auto fit" columnalign="center center right">
          <m:mtr>
            <m:mtd/>
            <m:mtd>
              <m:mi>A</m:mi>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mo>(</m:mo>
                <m:mtable>
                  <m:mtr><m:mtd><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mtd><m:mtd><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:mtd></m:mtr>
                  <m:mtr><m:mtd><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:mtd><m:mtd><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mtd></m:mtr>
                </m:mtable>
                <m:mo>)</m:mo>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:mtd>
            <m:mtd class="eqnum"/>
          </m:mtr>
        </m:mtable>
      </m:math>
    </informalequation>
    <para>Its eigenvalues <inlineequation><m:math><m:mi>λ</m:mi></m:math></inlineequation> are the
      roots of the characteristic polynomial:</para>
    <informalequation xml:id="eq-characteristic">
      <m:math display="block">
        <m:mtable width="100%" columnwidth="fit auto fit" columnalign="center center right">
          <m:mtr>
            <m:mtd/>
            <m:mtd>
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>det</m:mi><m:mo>&#x2061;</m:mo>
                <m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mi>A</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mi>λ</m:mi><m:mi>I</m:mi><m:mo>)</m:mo>
              </m:mrow>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mo>|</m:mo>
                <m:mtable>
                  <m:mtr>
                    <m:mtd><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mi>λ</m:mi></m:mtd>
                    <m:mtd><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:mtd>
                  </m:mtr>
                  <m:mtr>
                    <m:mtd><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:mtd>
                    <m:mtd><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mi>λ</m:mi></m:mtd>
                  </m:mtr>
                </m:mtable>
                <m:mo>|</m:mo>
              </m:mrow>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:msup><m:mrow><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mi>λ</m:mi><m:mo>)</m:mo></m:mrow><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup>
              <m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:msup><m:mi>λ</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mi>λ</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn>
            </m:mtd>
            <m:mtd class="eqnum"/>
          </m:mtr>
        </m:mtable>
      </m:math>
    </informalequation>
    <para>The quadratic formula gives the roots of any polynomial
      <inlineequation><m:math>
        <m:mi>a</m:mi><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mi>b</m:mi><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mi>c</m:mi>
      </m:math></inlineequation>:</para>
    <informalequation xml:id="eq-quadratic">
      <m:math display="block">
        <m:mtable width="100%" columnwidth="fit auto fit" columnalign="center center right">
          <m:mtr>
            <m:mtd/>
            <m:mtd>
              <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mfrac>
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mi>b</m:mi><m:mo>±</m:mo>
                  <m:msqrt><m:msup><m:mi>b</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mi>a</m:mi><m:mi>c</m:mi></m:msqrt>
                </m:mrow>
                <m:mrow><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mi>a</m:mi></m:mrow>
              </m:mfrac>
            </m:mtd>
            <m:mtd class="eqnum"/>
          </m:mtr>
        </m:mtable>
      </m:math>
    </informalequation>
    <para>For <xref linkend="eq-characteristic" xrefstyle="equation"/> it gives
      <inlineequation><m:math><m:mi>λ</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo>±</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo>)</m:mo><m:mo>/</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:math></inlineequation>,
      that is, <inlineequation><m:math><m:mi>λ</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:math></inlineequation>
      and <inlineequation><m:math><m:mi>λ</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:math></inlineequation>.
      A check with the vector
      <inlineequation><m:math>
        <m:msup>
          <m:mrow><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo>)</m:mo></m:mrow>
          <m:mi>T</m:mi>
        </m:msup>
      </m:math></inlineequation> confirms the larger one:</para>
    <informalequation xml:id="eq-eigen">
      <m:math display="block">
        <m:mtable width="100%" columnwidth="fit auto fit" columnalign="center center right">
          <m:mtr>
            <m:mtd/>
            <m:mtd>
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mo>(</m:mo>
                <m:mtable>
                  <m:mtr><m:mtd><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mtd><m:mtd><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:mtd></m:mtr>
                  <m:mtr><m:mtd><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:mtd><m:mtd><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mtd></m:mtr>
                </m:mtable>
                <m:mo>)</m:mo>
              </m:mrow>
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mo>(</m:mo>
                <m:mtable>
                  <m:mtr><m:mtd><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:mtd></m:mtr>
                  <m:mtr><m:mtd><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:mtd></m:mtr>
                </m:mtable>
                <m:mo>)</m:mo>
              </m:mrow>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mo>(</m:mo>
                <m:mtable>
                  <m:mtr><m:mtd><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mtd></m:mtr>
                  <m:mtr><m:mtd><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mtd></m:mtr>
                </m:mtable>
                <m:mo>)</m:mo>
              </m:mrow>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mn>3</m:mn>
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mo>(</m:mo>
                <m:mtable>
                  <m:mtr><m:mtd><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:mtd></m:mtr>
                  <m:mtr><m:mtd><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:mtd></m:mtr>
                </m:mtable>
                <m:mo>)</m:mo>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:mtd>
            <m:mtd class="eqnum"/>
          </m:mtr>
        </m:mtable>
      </m:math>
    </informalequation>
    <para>In the same way
      <inlineequation><m:math><m:msup><m:mrow><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mrow><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mo>)</m:mo></m:mrow><m:mi>T</m:mi></m:msup></m:math></inlineequation>
      is an eigenvector for
      <inlineequation><m:math><m:mi>λ</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:math></inlineequation>. The
      two eigenvectors are orthogonal, as they must be for a symmetric matrix.</para>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-binomial">
    <title>The binomial theorem</title>
    <para>Expanding the power of a sum gives the coefficients that Pascal’s triangle lists:</para>
    <informalequation xml:id="eq-binomial">
      <m:math display="block">
        <m:mtable width="100%" columnwidth="fit auto fit" columnalign="center center right">
          <m:mtr>
            <m:mtd/>
            <m:mtd>
              <m:msup>
                <m:mrow><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mi>a</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mi>b</m:mi><m:mo>)</m:mo></m:mrow>
                <m:mi>n</m:mi>
              </m:msup>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:munderover>
                <m:mo>∑</m:mo>
                <m:mrow><m:mi>k</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mrow>
                <m:mi>n</m:mi>
              </m:munderover>
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mo>(</m:mo>
                <m:mfrac linethickness="0"><m:mi>n</m:mi><m:mi>k</m:mi></m:mfrac>
                <m:mo>)</m:mo>
              </m:mrow>
              <m:msup><m:mi>a</m:mi><m:mrow><m:mi>n</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mi>k</m:mi></m:mrow></m:msup>
              <m:msup><m:mi>b</m:mi><m:mi>k</m:mi></m:msup>
            </m:mtd>
            <m:mtd class="eqnum"/>
          </m:mtr>
        </m:mtable>
      </m:math>
    </informalequation>
    <para>The binomial coefficient counts the subsets with
      <inlineequation><m:math><m:mi>k</m:mi></m:math></inlineequation> elements of a set with
      <inlineequation><m:math><m:mi>n</m:mi></m:math></inlineequation> elements:</para>
    <informalequation xml:id="eq-coefficient">
      <m:math display="block">
        <m:mtable width="100%" columnwidth="fit auto fit" columnalign="center center right">
          <m:mtr>
            <m:mtd/>
            <m:mtd>
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mo>(</m:mo>
                <m:mfrac linethickness="0"><m:mi>n</m:mi><m:mi>k</m:mi></m:mfrac>
                <m:mo>)</m:mo>
              </m:mrow>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mfrac>
                <m:mrow><m:mi>n</m:mi><m:mo>!</m:mo></m:mrow>
                <m:mrow><m:mi>k</m:mi><m:mo>!</m:mo><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mi>n</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mi>k</m:mi><m:mo>)</m:mo><m:mo>!</m:mo></m:mrow>
              </m:mfrac>
            </m:mtd>
            <m:mtd class="eqnum"/>
          </m:mtr>
        </m:mtable>
      </m:math>
    </informalequation>
    <para>Two choices of <inlineequation><m:math><m:mi>a</m:mi></m:math></inlineequation> and
      <inlineequation><m:math><m:mi>b</m:mi></m:math></inlineequation> in
      <xref linkend="eq-binomial" xrefstyle="equation"/> give identities that are worth remembering. With
      <inlineequation><m:math><m:mi>a</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mi>b</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:math></inlineequation>
      the coefficients add up to
      <inlineequation><m:math><m:msup><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mi>n</m:mi></m:msup></m:math></inlineequation>,
      the number of all subsets:</para>
    <informalequation xml:id="eq-subsets">
      <m:math display="block">
        <m:mtable width="100%" columnwidth="fit auto fit" columnalign="center center right">
          <m:mtr>
            <m:mtd/>
            <m:mtd>
              <m:munderover>
                <m:mo>∑</m:mo>
                <m:mrow><m:mi>k</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mrow>
                <m:mi>n</m:mi>
              </m:munderover>
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mo>(</m:mo>
                <m:mfrac linethickness="0"><m:mi>n</m:mi><m:mi>k</m:mi></m:mfrac>
                <m:mo>)</m:mo>
              </m:mrow>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:msup><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mi>n</m:mi></m:msup>
            </m:mtd>
            <m:mtd class="eqnum"/>
          </m:mtr>
        </m:mtable>
      </m:math>
    </informalequation>
    <para>With
      <inlineequation><m:math><m:mi>a</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:math></inlineequation> and
      <inlineequation><m:math><m:mi>b</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mrow><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></inlineequation>
      the signs alternate, and for
      <inlineequation><m:math><m:mi>n</m:mi><m:mo>≥</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:math></inlineequation> the
      sum is zero:</para>
    <informalequation xml:id="eq-alternating">
      <m:math display="block">
        <m:mtable width="100%" columnwidth="fit auto fit" columnalign="center center right">
          <m:mtr>
            <m:mtd/>
            <m:mtd>
              <m:munderover>
                <m:mo>∑</m:mo>
                <m:mrow><m:mi>k</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mrow>
                <m:mi>n</m:mi>
              </m:munderover>
              <m:msup><m:mrow><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mrow><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mo>)</m:mo></m:mrow><m:mi>k</m:mi></m:msup>
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mo>(</m:mo>
                <m:mfrac linethickness="0"><m:mi>n</m:mi><m:mi>k</m:mi></m:mfrac>
                <m:mo>)</m:mo>
              </m:mrow>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mn>0</m:mn>
            </m:mtd>
            <m:mtd class="eqnum"/>
          </m:mtr>
        </m:mtable>
      </m:math>
    </informalequation>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-e">
    <title>The number e</title>
    <para>The binomial theorem also leads to the number
      <inlineequation><m:math><m:mi>e</m:mi></m:math></inlineequation>. Take
      <inlineequation><m:math><m:mi>b</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo>/</m:mo><m:mi>n</m:mi></m:math></inlineequation>
      and <inlineequation><m:math><m:mi>a</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:math></inlineequation>
      in <xref linkend="eq-binomial" xrefstyle="equation"/>: the coefficient of each power grows towards
      <inlineequation><m:math><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo>/</m:mo><m:mi>k</m:mi><m:mo>!</m:mo></m:math></inlineequation>
      as <inlineequation><m:math><m:mi>n</m:mi></m:math></inlineequation> grows, and in the
      limit</para>
    <informalequation xml:id="eq-e-limit">
      <m:math display="block">
        <m:mtable width="100%" columnwidth="fit auto fit" columnalign="center center right">
          <m:mtr>
            <m:mtd/>
            <m:mtd>
              <m:mi>e</m:mi>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:munder>
                <m:mi>lim</m:mi>
                <m:mrow><m:mi>n</m:mi><m:mo>→</m:mo><m:mi>∞</m:mi></m:mrow>
              </m:munder>
              <m:msup>
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mo>(</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mi>n</m:mi></m:mfrac>
                  <m:mo>)</m:mo>
                </m:mrow>
                <m:mi>n</m:mi>
              </m:msup>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:munderover>
                <m:mo>∑</m:mo>
                <m:mrow><m:mi>k</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mrow>
                <m:mi>∞</m:mi>
              </m:munderover>
              <m:mfrac>
                <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                <m:mrow><m:mi>k</m:mi><m:mo>!</m:mo></m:mrow>
              </m:mfrac>
            </m:mtd>
            <m:mtd class="eqnum"/>
          </m:mtr>
        </m:mtable>
      </m:math>
    </informalequation>
    <para>so that
      <inlineequation><m:math><m:mi>e</m:mi><m:mo>≈</m:mo><m:mn>2.71828</m:mn></m:math></inlineequation>.
      Both expressions converge, but the series is far faster: its seventh partial sum already has
      four correct decimals.</para>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-numbers">
    <title>Checking the numbers</title>
    <para>The table lists the partial sums of the series of <xref linkend="eq-basel" xrefstyle="equation"/> and their
      distance to the limit
      <inlineequation><m:math><m:msup><m:mi>π</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>/</m:mo><m:mn>6</m:mn><m:mo>≈</m:mo><m:mn>1.6449</m:mn></m:math></inlineequation>.
      The error shrinks like
      <inlineequation><m:math><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo>/</m:mo><m:mi>n</m:mi></m:math></inlineequation>,
      and every row respects the bound of <xref linkend="eq-bound" xrefstyle="equation"/>.</para>
    <table>
      <title>Partial sums of the series for ζ(2)</title>
      <tgroup cols="4">
